126→      <div class="glance-title">At a Glance</div>
127→      <div class="glance-sections">
128→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's working:</strong> You&apos;ve built a genuinely impressive multi-agent workflow where Claude orchestrates specialist agents, manages sub-tasks, and posts code review comments on PRs — this is power-user territory. You&apos;re also closing the loop by having Claude codify session learnings into skill files and automation hooks, which compounds your productivity over time. Your persistence through large debugging campaigns (like fixing 28 test failures across commits) shows you know how to keep Claude accountable to verification. <a href="#section-wins" class="see-more">Impressive Things You Did →</a></div>
129→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>What's hindering you:</strong> On Claude&apos;s side, it consistently drifts into building infrastructure and planning when you need it executing — your GPU competition sessions lost hours to frameworks and meta-work before touching actual submissions, and initial code often ships with correctness bugs or silent fallbacks that burn debugging time. On your side, Claude frequently pursued the wrong approach until you intervened mid-session; setting tighter upfront constraints (explicit &apos;do not do&apos; lists, concrete deliverables, and time boundaries) would catch these drifts before they cost you hours. <a href="#section-friction" class="see-more">Where Things Go Wrong →</a></div>
130→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Quick wins to try:</strong> Try setting up hooks for pre-commit linting and test validation — you&apos;ve already started building auto-lint hooks manually, but formalizing them would catch buggy code before it lands. Also, for your GPU competition and vault-keeper workflows, consider using headless mode to run submission pipelines and maintenance cycles from scripts rather than interactive sessions where Claude tends to over-plan. <a href="#section-features" class="see-more">Features to Try →</a></div>
131→        <div class="glance-section"><strong>Ambitious workflows:</strong> As models get more capable, your multi-agent orchestration pattern is perfectly positioned for parallel specialist agents with strict timeboxes — imagine spawning a benchmarking agent, a kernel-writing agent, and a submission agent simultaneously, each scoped to deliver in 30 minutes instead of one agent wandering for two hours. Your vault-keeper and codebase maintenance workflows should also become fully autonomous daemons that run on a schedule, detect drift, and self-heal without you triggering each pass. <a href="#section-horizon" class="see-more">On the Horizon →</a></div>
