802→          <div class="horizon-possible">With 22 &apos;wrong approach&apos; friction events and sessions where Claude spent hours on infrastructure instead of execution, you need guardrails that force iterative progress against concrete test suites. Autonomous agents can implement features in tight loops—write code, run tests, fix failures, repeat—shipping verified changes without human babysitting.</div>
803→          <div class="horizon-tip"><strong>Getting started:</strong> Use Claude Code&apos;s sub-agent spawning (you&apos;re already using 108 Agent calls) combined with CLAUDE.md rules that mandate test execution before any commit. Define exit criteria upfront so agents can&apos;t spiral into planning mode.</div>
804→          <div class="pattern-prompt"><div class="prompt-label">Paste into Claude Code:</div><code>Implement the following feature using a strict test-driven loop. RULES: 1) Write or identify the relevant test suite FIRST. 2) Make the smallest code change that could fix/advance a failing test. 3) Run the full test suite after every change. 4) Never spend more than 3 iterations on a single approach—if stuck, document what failed and try an alternative. 5) Do NOT enter planning mode or build infrastructure unless a test specifically requires it. 6) Commit after each green test suite run with a descriptive message. Feature: [DESCRIPTION]. Tests located in: [PATH]. Stop when all tests pass and push the branch.</code><button class="copy-btn" onclick="copyText(this)">Copy</button></div>
